
𝗜 𝗳𝗶𝗻𝗮𝗹𝗹𝘆 𝘂𝗻𝗱𝗲𝗿𝘀𝘁𝗮𝗻𝗱 𝘄𝗵𝗮𝘁 𝗵𝗮𝗽𝗽𝗲𝗻𝘀 𝘁𝗵𝗲 𝗺𝗼𝗺𝗲𝗻𝘁 `𝗻𝗲𝘄 𝗦𝘁𝘂𝗱𝗲𝗻𝘁()` 𝗶𝘀 𝗰𝗮𝗹𝗹𝗲𝗱.
It's the Constructor.
And it's more interesting than I thought. Here's what I learned 👇

🔹𝗗𝗲𝗳𝗮𝘂𝗹𝘁 𝘃𝗮𝗹𝘂𝗲𝘀 𝗲𝘅𝗶𝘀𝘁 𝗯𝗲𝗳𝗼𝗿𝗲 𝘆𝗼𝘂 𝗱𝗼 𝗮𝗻𝘆𝘁𝗵𝗶𝗻𝗴
Java assigns 0 to ints, null to Strings. The object is already "alive" even before your constructor runs.

🔹 𝗖𝗼𝗻𝘀𝘁𝗿𝘂𝗰𝘁𝗼𝗿𝘀 𝗵𝗮𝘃𝗲 𝗮 𝘀𝘁𝗿𝗶𝗰𝘁 𝗿𝘂𝗹𝗲𝘀𝗲𝘁
Same name as the class. No return type. Not even void. Break one rule and it stops being a constructor.

🔹 𝗧𝗵𝗲 𝘁𝗿𝗮𝗽 𝗺𝗼𝘀𝘁 𝗯𝗲𝗴𝗶𝗻𝗻𝗲𝗿𝘀 𝗳𝗮𝗹𝗹 𝗶𝗻𝘁𝗼
The moment you write a parameterized constructor, Java removes the default one. Silently. No warning. Your `new Student()` call suddenly breaks.

🔹 𝗖𝗼𝗻𝘀𝘁𝗿𝘂𝗰𝘁𝗼𝗿 𝗰𝗵𝗮𝗶𝗻𝗶𝗻𝗴 𝗶𝘀 𝘂𝗻𝗱𝗲𝗿𝗿𝗮𝘁𝗲𝗱
Using this() to call one constructor from another. One source of truth. No copy-pasted logic. Cleaner than anything I was writing before.

🔹 𝗬𝗼𝘂 𝗰𝗮𝗻𝗻𝗼𝘁 𝗰𝗮𝗹𝗹 𝗮 𝗰𝗼𝗻𝘀𝘁𝗿𝘂𝗰𝘁𝗼𝗿 𝗺𝗮𝗻𝘂𝗮𝗹𝗹𝘆
`s.Student()` is illegal. It only fires on new or via this(). That's it.

𝗝𝗮𝘃𝗮 𝗶𝘀 𝗡𝗢𝗧 𝗽𝗮𝘀𝘀-𝗯𝘆-𝗿𝗲𝗳𝗲𝗿𝗲𝗻𝗰𝗲.

𝗜 𝘁𝗵𝗼𝘂𝗴𝗵𝘁 𝗶𝘁 𝘄𝗮𝘀. 𝗙𝗼𝗿 𝗺𝗼𝗻𝘁𝗵𝘀.

𝗛𝗲𝗿𝗲'𝘀 𝘁𝗵𝗲 𝘁𝗿𝘂𝘁𝗵 𝗶𝗻 𝟯 𝗹𝗶𝗻𝗲𝘀:

→ Object lives in Heap. Reference lives in Stack.
→ You pass a copy of the address — not the object.
→ Modify fields ✅ affects original. Reassign reference ❌ does nothing.

One wrong mental model.
Two hours of debugging a bug that didn't exist.
One copy constructor that fixed everything.

The machine always knows what it's doing.
The question is — do you?
